Grid bugs don't even leave corpses.  The thing is, there has to
be _some_ kind of monster on dungeon level 1, and the player
at that point may not be capable of dealing with any actual
threat.  This depends somewhat on role.  A level-one Valkyrie
could probably handle something that can do actual damage
(albeit not a great deal of it), but some roles can start with
single-digit numbers for almost everything, including strength
and hitpoints.  They might have 18 charisma to compensate,
but that's of limited practical use on dungeon level one.

But leprechauns aren't a dungeon-level-one monster.

I won't go so far as to categorically agree with the
blanket statement that non-threats aren't interesting,
but I do think leprechauns have a (marginally) more
interesting effect on game play as they stand now.
Not that they're the _most_ interesting monster in
the dungeon, or anything.

Actually, if I wanted to make leprechauns more
interesting, I think I'd give them a percentage chance
(maybe 10% or so) of getting a semi-random wand
(e.g., if they make the 10% cut: 50% chance of a
wand of teleport; failing that, 50% chance of a wand
of digging; failing that, 50% chance of a wand of
light; failing that, a wand of make invisible -- or
something along those lines).  Come to think of
it, maybe they would also be a good candidate
for gaining the ability to break wands as a form
of suicidal offensive tactic.

I don't think it's necessary or desirable to implement
all legends into the game.  There are also legends that
if you catch a leprechaun he has to grant you a wish,
but the game doesn't need more sources of wishes.


